### Version

Which version(s) did you encounter this bug ? 4.2.6

### Context

In http1xClientConnection.handleHttpMessage() the code throws a "Received HTTP message with no request in progress" exception when a response is received without a request in progress. In this case, the content of a DefaultHttpContent is a byteBuf and hold data.  Per netty's reference counted objects documentation, the code should release the byteBuf buffer before throwing the exception, as nothing will access it anymore.
_The general rule of thumb is that the party that accesses a reference-counted object last is also responsible for the destruction of that reference-counted object. Specifically:

    If a [sending] component is supposed to pass a reference-counted object to another [receiving] component, the sending component usually does not need to destroy it but defers that decision to the receiving component.
    If a component consumes a reference-counted object and knows nothing else will access it anymore (i.e., does not pass along a reference to yet another component), the component should destroy it._


### Do you have a reproducer?
The code was ran in Http1xTest class in the vertx code.
Updating requestNum to big enough of a value will create an out of memory exception. Current code will show the increase in used Direct Memory 

```
@Test
  public void testMemoryLeak() {

    String longParam = TestUtils.randomAlphaString(5000);
    String content = TestUtils.randomAlphaString(1000000);
    NetServer server = vertx.createNetServer();
    AtomicBoolean isConnected = new AtomicBoolean(false);
    final int requestNum = 10000;
    AtomicInteger requestCounter = new AtomicInteger(requestNum);
    ByteBufAllocatorMetricProvider unpooledMetricProvider =
      (ByteBufAllocatorMetricProvider) VertxByteBufAllocator.POOLED_ALLOCATOR;
    PooledByteBufAllocatorMetric metric = (PooledByteBufAllocatorMetric) unpooledMetricProvider.metric();

    long prevUsedDirectMemory = metric.usedDirectMemory();
    long firstUsedDirectMemoryAllocation = 0;


    server.connectHandler(so -> {
      System.out.println("server.connectHandler");
      for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
        so.write("HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\n" +
          "Content-Type: text/plain\r\n" +
          "Content-Length: " + content.length() + "\r\n" +
          "\r\n" +
          content);

      }
    });


    try {
      server.listen(testAddress, onSuccess(v -> {
        isConnected.set(true);

        }));
      while(!isConnected.get()) {
          try {
            Thread.sleep(100);
          } catch (Exception e) {

          }
      }
      //System.out.println("Server is connected");

      for (int i = 0 ; i < requestNum; i++) {
        vertx.createHttpClient(new HttpClientOptions().setMaxInitialLineLength(6000))
          .request(new RequestOptions(requestOptions).setURI("/?t=" + longParam))
          .compose(HttpClientRequest::send)
          .onComplete(onSuccess(resp -> {
            resp.bodyHandler(body -> {
              //System.out.println("Received response");
              requestCounter.decrementAndGet();

            });
          }));
        try {
          Thread.sleep(10);
        } catch (Exception e) {

        }
        long curUsedDirectMemory = metric.usedDirectMemory();
        if(curUsedDirectMemory != prevUsedDirectMemory) {
          System.out.println("Request #" + i + " curUsedDirectMemory (" + curUsedDirectMemory +
            ") is different from startUsedDirectMemory (" + prevUsedDirectMemory + ")");
          if(prevUsedDirectMemory == 0) {
            firstUsedDirectMemoryAllocation = curUsedDirectMemory;
          }
          prevUsedDirectMemory = curUsedDirectMemory;
        }

      }

      while(requestCounter.get() > 0) {
        System.out.println("requestCounter = " + requestCounter.get());
        try {
          Thread.sleep(100);
        } catch (Exception e) {

        }
      }

      testComplete();
      await(90, TimeUnit.MINUTES);
      System.out.println("Stopped waiting");
    } finally {
      System.out.println("Closing server");
      server.close();
    }
    System.out.println("First used direct memory allocation: " + firstUsedDirectMemoryAllocation);
    System.out.println("Final used direct memory allocation: " + prevUsedDirectMemory);
    assertEquals("Direct memory allocation difference", firstUsedDirectMemoryAllocation, prevUsedDirectMemory);
  }
```

### Steps to reproduce

1. Create a test where the client receives a HTTP response with no request in progress multiple times.


### Extra


* Running on java17
* Reproducible in both Mac Sonoma and Linux.
